
  One thing determines almost every decision in piece #1, so it's the right next question.

  ---

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether tasks are shared between people or belong to one person. Your original request says "users get
  notified when tasks [3mthey care about[0m change" — which strongly implies changes made by [3msomeone else[0m. If that's right,
  this is a multi-user app, and that decision reaches all the way down into piece #1.

  - [1mMulti-user, shared tasks[0m — requires a server, a database, and real user accounts from day one. Buys you the
    notification story you actually asked for. Costs the most upfront, and the storage/auth choices are expensive to
    reverse later.
  - [1mSingle-user, local-first[0m — no server, no accounts, browser storage. Cheap and fast. But it rules out your stated
    goal: with one user, nothing changes behind your back, so "notifications" degrades into due-date reminders — a
    different feature wearing the same name.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user later[0m — start local, add a backend when needed. Tempting, but migrating storage and
    retrofitting auth is the classic expensive rewrite. Only worth it if you're genuinely unsure the app has legs.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m multi-user with a server. Your request has other people changing your tasks baked into its premise,
  and that's the one assumption that's painful to add after the fact. If I've misread and this is a personal task list,
  say so — it makes everything downstream much smaller.
